/-!
# Nullstellensatz with a cardinal bound on generators

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 00FU, `theorem-uncountable-nullstellensatz`, clauses (1) and (2)
(blueprint: lem-sfb-cardinal-generator-nullstellensatz).

Generation is expressed by surjectivity of multivariate polynomial evaluation. Cardinalities
are lifted to a common universe; the coefficient field, algebra and generator index type
may lie in independent universes.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v w

namespace Algebra

/-- An algebra generated by fewer elements than the cardinality of its coefficient field
has algebraic maximal quotients and is Jacobson. No nontriviality of the algebra or
infinitude of the field or index type is assumed.

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 00FU, `theorem-uncountable-nullstellensatz`, clauses (1) and (2)
(blueprint: lem-sfb-cardinal-generator-nullstellensatz).

Proof plan:
1. For finite `I`, generation gives a finite type algebra
   (def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra). The finite-type Jacobson theorem
   (lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-field-prime-intersection-maximal) gives the second clause.
   Every maximal quotient is a field (lem-weil-common-extension-fields-quotient-field)
   and is finite-dimensional by lem-weil-pbc-zariski-lemma. A spanning set of size `d`
   forces a relation among `1, x, ..., x^d` by
   lem-sfb-independent-family-spanning-cardinality, proving algebraicity.
2. For infinite `I`, in any field generated by `I` the monomials span, by
   def-weil-pbc-mv-polynomial-ring. Their index set has cardinality `#I` by
   lem-sfb-monomial-index-cardinality. A transcendental element gives an independent
   family of reciprocals indexed by `F` by lem-sfb-transcendental-reciprocals-independent.
   The independent/spanning comparison lem-sfb-independent-family-spanning-cardinality
   would imply `#F ≤ #I`, contrary to the cardinal hypothesis.
3. Apply this field claim to every maximal quotient: polynomial evaluation descends
   surjectively to the quotient, which is a field by
   lem-weil-common-extension-fields-quotient-field. For the zero algebra there are no
   maximal or prime ideals, so both conclusions hold vacuously.
4. To prove the Jacobson clause for infinite `I`, fix a prime `p` and `f ∉ p`.
   The localization `B = (A/p)[1/f]` (def-weil-ring-localization-away) is nonzero:
   otherwise a power of the nonzero image of `f` would vanish in the domain `A/p`.
   Choose a maximal ideal of `B` (lem-weil-common-extension-fields-maximal-ideal).
   Its quotient field `L` is generated by the images of the original generators and
   `f⁻¹`. Adding one index preserves the infinite cardinal `#I`, so step 2 makes `L/F`
   algebraic (lem-weil-common-extension-fields-quotient-field supplies its field structure).
5. The image `C` of `A` in `L` contains `F` and is a field: for nonzero `c ∈ C`, remove
   initial zero coefficients from an algebraic relation and cancel the resulting power
   of `c` to obtain a relation with nonzero constant coefficient. Solve that relation
   for `c⁻¹` as a polynomial in `c`. Thus the kernel of `A → L` is maximal, contains
   `p`, and omits `f`, whose image is a unit.
6. Every maximal ideal containing `p` contains its elements. Conversely, step 5 supplies
   such a maximal ideal omitting each `f ∉ p`. Their intersection is therefore exactly
   `p`, which is the Jacobson property.
-/
theorem nullstellensatz_of_cardinal_generators
    {F : Type u} [Field F] {A : Type v} [CommRing A] [Algebra F A]
    {I : Type w} (a : I → A)
    (hgen : Function.Surjective (MvPolynomial.aeval a : MvPolynomial I F →ₐ[F] A))
    (hcard : Cardinal.lift.{u} (Cardinal.mk I) < Cardinal.lift.{w} (Cardinal.mk F)) :
    (∀ (m : Ideal A), m.IsMaximal → Algebra.IsAlgebraic F (A ⧸ m)) ∧ IsJacobsonRing A := by
  sorry

end Algebra
